Adding Custom Syntax
********************

.[perex]
GitHub extended Markdown with tables and task lists because its users needed them. You can do the same: teach Markdown or Texy syntax `@@jane` mentions, tip boxes or abbreviations that explain themselves. All it takes is a regular expression that recognizes the syntax and a function that turns it into an element of the document.

This page is about a completely new syntax. If you only want to render something Texy already knows in a different way, such as an image or a heading, see [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers].


Mentions in Comments
====================

On our blog, we want `@@jane` in a comment to become a link to the user's profile:

```
Thanks, @@jane, I'll give it a try.
```

```html
<p>Thanks, <a href="/user/jane">@jane</a>, I'll give it a try.</p>
```

How much code does it take? A single call:

```php
use Texy\Nodes;
use Texy\Parsing\ParseContext;

$mention = function (ParseContext $context, array $matches, array $offsets): Nodes\LinkNode {
	$name = $matches[1];
	return new Nodes\LinkNode(
		url: '/user/' . $name,
		content: Nodes\ContentNode::fromText('@' . $name),
		range: new Texy\Range($offsets[0], strlen($matches[0])),
	);
};

$comments = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$comments->addInlineSyntax('blog/mention', '~@@([a-z0-9_]+)~i', $mention);
$html = $comments->toHtml($comment);  // $comment is the text of the comment
```

`addInlineSyntax()` takes three things:

- the **name** of the syntax, here `blog/mention`. You can use it to turn the syntax off (`$comments->allowedSyntax['blog/mention'] = false`). A prefix like `blog/` ensures the name never clashes with a syntax added by a future version of Texy.
- a **regular expression** that recognizes the syntax in the text. Texy adds only the `u` flag for UTF-8 to it, so it behaves exactly as it does when you try it in any regular expression tester.
- a **function** that turns the matched text into a document node.

The function receives the matched text and its regular expression groups in `$matches` (`$matches[1]` is the name), and their positions in the text in `$offsets`. It returns a node, here a link (`LinkNode`) whose text `@jane` is created by `ContentNode::fromText()`. From the positions we build a `Texy\Range`, the range of text the node came from. The HTML does not depend on it, but tools that work with the source text use it to find where the mention sits in the text.

As its fourth parameter, the function receives the name of the syntax, so one function can handle several syntaxes. A group that did not participate in the match is `null` in both `$matches` and `$offsets`.

Because we return a link, a node Texy already knows, we don't have to worry about how it gets rendered. It renders like any other link, in HTML as well as in plain text.

The syntax works exactly the same way in Texy syntax; you just register it on `$texy`:

```php
$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$texy->addInlineSyntax('blog/mention', '~@@([a-z0-9_]+)~i', $mention);
```

Register the syntax before you start converting text.


Tip Boxes
=========

A mention sits inside a line. A syntax that takes up whole lines is registered as a block syntax. In our articles, we want boxes with a tip or a warning:

```
:::tip
Dry them in a **thin** layer and [turn them](https://example.com) regularly.
:::
```

```html
<div class="box box-tip">
	<p>Dry them in a <strong>thin</strong> layer and <a
	href="https://example.com">turn them</a> regularly.</p>
</div>
```

```php
$box = function (ParseContext $context, array $matches, array $offsets): Nodes\DivNode {
	return new Nodes\DivNode(
		$context->parseBlock($matches[2], $offsets[2]),
		new Texy\Modifier(classes: ['box' => true, 'box-' . $matches[1] => true]),
		new Texy\Range($offsets[0], strlen($matches[0])),
	);
};

$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;  // articles
$markdown->config->locale = 'en';       // typography rules of the articles' language
$markdown->addBlockSyntax('blog/box', '~^:::(tip|warning)\n(.+?)\n:::$~s', $box);
```

A block expression must start with `^`, the start of a line, and Texy adds the `m` flag to it automatically, so that `^` and `$` mean the start and end of a line.

Inside the box, we want to write the same way as anywhere else in the article, with bold text and links. That is why we don't insert the content as text but have it parsed by calling `$context->parseBlock()`. We get its nodes and put them into a `DivNode`, the node for a generic `<div>` block. Its classes come from a `Texy\Modifier`, the same object that a [modifier |syntax#Modifiers] in the text produces. The second parameter of `parseBlock()`, `$offsets[2]`, says where the inner text starts, so that its nodes get correct positions too. For content within a line, there is the analogous `$context->parseInline()`.

We write the box in Markdown, so its content is parsed as Markdown. If you register the same function on `$texy`, the content is parsed as Texy syntax.


A New Block Type in Texy Syntax
-------------------------------

Texy syntax has its own construct for blocks, `/--type … \--`, such as `/--div` or `/--html`. The box fits into it without any regular expression; all you need to do is handle the new type in the `delimitedBlockHandler` function:

```php
$texy->config->delimitedBlockHandler = fn(ParseContext $context, string $type, string $content, ?string $param, ?Texy\Modifier $modifier, Closure $next) => match ($type) {
	'tip' => new Nodes\DivNode(
		$context->parseBlock($content),
		new Texy\Modifier(classes: ['box' => true, 'box-tip' => true]),
	),
	default => $next(),
};
```

```texy
/--tip
Dry them in a **thin** layer.
\--
```

There is one function for all `/--` blocks, and Texy calls it for each of them, built-in and unknown alike. It receives the type in lowercase (an empty string for a bare `/--`), the content of the block with the common indentation removed, the text written after the type (`/--tip something`) and the modifier of the block. Texy works out the positions by itself. The function passes other types to `$next()`, which returns whatever Texy itself would make of the block. When the function returns `null`, the block stays as text.

In the same way, you can replace a built-in type, such as `code`, with your own node, or handle `/--php` as a code block. A block whose type nobody handles is shown by Texy exactly as written, including the `/--type` and `\--` lines. A built-in type turned off in `$texy->allowedSyntax` never reaches the function; turning off your own types is up to the function.


Rejecting a Match
=================

The function doesn't have to accept every match. When it returns `null`, Texy leaves the text alone and gives the other syntaxes a chance. This is useful when the regular expression matches more than you want. Abbreviations we know should explain themselves, while other words in capital letters should stay plain text:

```php
$abbreviations = [
	'CSS' => 'Cascading Style Sheets',
	'HTML' => 'HyperText Markup Language',
];

$markdown->addInlineSyntax('blog/abbr', '~\b([A-Z]{2,})\b~', function (ParseContext $context, array $matches, array $offsets) use ($abbreviations): ?Nodes\AnnotationNode {
	if (!isset($abbreviations[$matches[1]])) {
		return null; // unknown abbreviation, the text stays as it is
	}
	return new Nodes\AnnotationNode(
		$matches[1],
		$abbreviations[$matches[1]],
		new Texy\Range($offsets[0], strlen($matches[0])),
	);
});
```

```html
<p>A website in <abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr> and <abbr
title="Cascading Style Sheets">CSS</abbr>, but not ABC.</p>
```

`AnnotationNode` is text with an explanation; Texy renders it as `<abbr>` with the explanation in its title.

The function can also return a `Nodes\ContentNode` with several nodes, which are inserted one after another instead of a single node. An empty `ContentNode` consumes the matched text and inserts nothing, which is handy for a syntax that only collects data.

With a block syntax, however, `null` costs more: a rejected block also swallows the places where the same expression would have found another block, and those then stay plain text. So write the conditions for rejecting a block straight into the regular expression. This applies to Markdown and Texy syntax alike.


When Syntaxes Overlap
=====================

Several syntaxes can match the same stretch of text. Texy picks the one that starts first. If several start at the same position in a line, the longer one wins; with the same length, your syntax beats a built-in one, and among yours the one registered earlier wins. For block syntaxes, length does not matter: yours beats a built-in one outright, and among yours the one registered earlier wins. This applies to Markdown and Texy syntax alike. The best defense is an expression that recognizes only what you really want. A mention with two at signs will never be mistaken for an email address.

If your syntax should lose in a particular situation, have the function return `null`, and Texy gives the syntaxes after it a chance.

.[note]
You can also register a syntax under the name of a built-in syntax (the constants `Texy\Syntax` and `Texy\Markdown\Syntax`) and thereby replace it. The replacement lasts for the whole lifetime of the object.


Custom Node
===========

Most of the time, the nodes Texy knows are enough, and you get their rendering for free. When none fits, you write your own node and a renderer that renders it. Say you want mentions to have their own node in the tree, holding the user's name. We register the syntax just as at the start of the page, only the function returns a new `MentionNode`:

```php
use Texy\Output\Html;

class MentionNode extends Nodes\InlineNode
{
	public function __construct(
		public string $name,
		public Nodes\ContentNode $content,
		public ?Texy\Range $range = null,
	) {
	}


	public function &getChildren(): Generator
	{
		yield $this->content;
	}
}

$comments->addInlineSyntax('blog/mention', '~@@([a-z0-9_]+)~i', fn(ParseContext $context, array $matches, array $offsets) => new MentionNode(
	$matches[1],
	Nodes\ContentNode::fromText('@' . $matches[1]),
	new Texy\Range($offsets[0], strlen($matches[0])),
));

$comments->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (MentionNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, ?Closure $previous) {
	$el = new Html\Element('a', ['href' => '/user/' . $node->name, 'class' => 'mention']);
	$el->setText('@' . $node->name);
	return $el;
});
```

```html
<p>Thanks, <a href="/user/jane" class="mention">@jane</a>.</p>
```

The `getChildren()` method returns the content of the node. When the HTML or plain-text output has no renderer for the node, it renders just its content, so the plain text will be `Thanks, @jane.`. Markdown itself has no `toText()` method; you get plain text from it by calling `(new Texy\Output\Text\Renderer)->render($document)` on the document after `transform()`. Printing back to source text, such as with `toTexy()`, however, throws an exception for a node without a renderer, as described in [Source Text Tools |source-tools#Replacing a Whole Element]. How to write renderers is described in [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers#Different HTML for an Element].


Differences in Markdown
=======================

Markdown parses text in two steps: first the blocks and only then the content of the lines, because a link can refer to a definition that comes later in the text. For custom block syntaxes, this leads to one rule: `$context->parseInline()` in a block function only sets the text aside, and Markdown parses it in the second step. You can put the result into a node, just don't read it as a finished tree inside the function. `$context->parseBlock()` works as usual, as in the box above.


Tips
====

- **Return nodes Texy knows** whenever you can. They render in every output, in HTML as well as in plain text.
- **Use prefixed names**, such as `blog/mention` or `acme/box`, so they never clash with the built-in ones and can be turned off via `allowedSyntax`.
- **Fill in the `Range`** from `$offsets`, the whole range the syntax takes up. Source text tools use it to find where the element lies and can, for example, replace it as a whole.
- **Try combinations.** What happens when you write the syntax inside a link, a blockquote or a code block?

Coming from Texy 3, where syntaxes were registered with `registerLinePattern()`? How to convert them is described in [Upgrading from Texy 3 |upgrading].

